---
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
import { PROFILE } from '@/data/site';
import model from '@/data/t20-model.json';

const m = model.metrics;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
const labels: Record<string, string> = {
  batting_team: 'Batting team (label-encoded)', bowling_team: 'Bowling team (label-encoded)', venue: 'Venue (label-encoded)',
  over: 'Overs completed', current_score: 'Current score', wickets: 'Wickets', current_run_rate: 'Current run rate', last_5_overs_runs: 'Runs in last 5 overs',
};
const maxAbs = Math.max(...model.coef.map((c) => Math.abs(c)));
---
<Page title={`T20 Score Predictor demo | ${PROFILE.name}`} description="Try my T20 score model live in the browser, and see what it actually learned from simulated match data.">
  <header class="hero-page fade">
    <div class="crumb"><a href="/">Portfolio</a> / <a href="/#projects">Projects</a> / T20 demo</div>
    <h1>T20 Score <span class="g">Predictor</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">My Linear Regression model, running live in your browser. Set the match situation and it predicts the final innings score.</p>
    <p class="t20-note"><b>Trained on simulated data.</b> The 600 IPL-style matches come from the project's own data generator, so treat predictions as a demo of the pipeline, not real forecasts.</p>
  </header>

  <form class="t20 fade" id="t20-form">
    <div class="t20-grid">
      <label>Batting team<select id="t20-bat">{model.encoders.batting_team.map((t, i) => <option value={i} selected={t === 'Mumbai Indians'}>{t}</option>)}</select></label>
      <label>Bowling team<select id="t20-bowl">{model.encoders.bowling_team.map((t, i) => <option value={i} selected={t === 'Chennai Super Kings'}>{t}</option>)}</select></label>
      <label class="wide">Venue<select id="t20-venue">{model.encoders.venue.map((t, i) => <option value={i} selected={t === 'Wankhede Stadium'}>{t}</option>)}</select></label>
      <label>Overs completed <output id="t20-over-v">10</output><input id="t20-over" type="range" min="1" max="20" value="10" /></label>
      <label>Wickets down <output id="t20-wk-v">2</output><input id="t20-wk" type="range" min="0" max="9" value="2" /></label>
      <label>Current score<input id="t20-score" type="number" min="0" max="300" value="87" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
      <label>Runs in last 5 overs<input id="t20-last5" type="number" min="0" max="150" value="43" inputmode="numeric" /></label>
    </div>
    <div class="t20-result" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="t20-big"><span>Predicted final score</span><b id="t20-pred">—</b></div>
      <div class="t20-side">
        <div><span>Current run rate</span><b id="t20-crr">—</b></div>
        <div><span>No-model baseline</span><b id="t20-naive">—</b></div>
        <div><span>Model vs baseline</span><b id="t20-diff">—</b></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </form>

  <article class="prose fade">
    <h2>What the model actually learned</h2>
    <p>I retrained the model with the project's own code and settings (seed 42, an 80/20 split of {model.rows.toLocaleString('en-US')} innings snapshots), then compared it with a baseline that does no learning at all: current score plus run rate times overs left.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Mean error (runs)</th><th>R²</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>{fmt(m.lr.mae)}</td><td>{m.lr.r2.toFixed(3)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Random Forest (100 trees)</td><td>{fmt(m.rf.mae)}</td><td>{m.rf.r2.toFixed(3)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No model: run-rate extrapolation</td><td>{fmt(m.naive_run_rate.mae)}</td><td>{m.naive_run_rate.r2.toFixed(3)}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>The regression ties the no-model baseline, and the forest doesn't beat it. That's the honest result: the simulated final scores were generated by extrapolating the run rate, so the best any model can do is rediscover that formula. You can see it in the weights below. Almost everything rides on the current run rate (about ×{model.coef[6]!.toFixed(1)}, roughly the 20 overs of an innings), while team and venue barely register.</p>
    <ul class="t20-coefs">
      {model.features.map((f, i) => (
        <li>
          <span>{labels[f] ?? f}</span>
          <i style={`--w:${(Math.abs(model.coef[i]!) / maxAbs * 100).toFixed(1)}%`}></i>
          <code>{model.coef[i]!.toFixed(3)}</code>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>The lesson I took from it: a high R² on synthetic data says more about the generator than the model. The next step is real ball-by-ball data, where team form, venue and wickets in hand have to earn their weight. Teams and venues should also be one-hot encoded instead of label-encoded, so the model doesn't treat them as ordered numbers.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/divyanshubhatiwal/t20-score-predictor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source code on GitHub ↗</a></p>
  </article>

  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="t20-model" set:html={JSON.stringify({ coef: model.coef, intercept: model.intercept })}></script>
  <script>
    import '@/scripts/t20';
  </script>
</Page>
